on a sunny tuesday afternoon on the  northeastern outskirts of paris 100  passengers began the boarding process  onto the world's fastest commercial  plane  navigating down the narrow body of the  concorde with pairs of business class  seats flanking them on each side  [Music]  flight 4590 was a charitable flight  carrying the predominantly german  passengers to embark on an adventurous  journey through the caribbean the panama  canal and finally ecuador aboard the ms  deutschland  the crew of the flight was composed of  the captain a 53 year old pilot with  over 300 hours flown in the concorde the  first officer was a 50 year old with  over 2 000 hours of concord flight time  and the flight engineer who again  accumulated over 900 hours of flying  with the concorde  at 2 34 pm ground control gave clearance  for the flight to take off from runway  26r the longest runway at charles de  gaulle airport which was needed to get  the concorde's cumbersome delta wings up  to take off speed  at 2 40 pm flight air france 4590 lined  up at the start of runway 26r beginning  the exciting takeoff sequence the  world's only commercial plane to employ  after burners  20 seconds later the first officer calls  out that they've reached 100 knots  six seconds later the officer calls out  v1 the point of no return aborting the  take-off now was no longer an option as  there was not enough runway left to stop  in time  a mere two seconds later a loud crash is  heard from the left wing and flames are  seen billowing from the plane crashing  just moments later into a nearby hotel  what followed was a multi-year air crash  investigation unraveling the cause of  the accident from the wreckage using  engineering  this is its story  [Music]  the challenge facing air crash  investigators for any tragic loss like  this is immense the concorde had crashed  into a small hotel and restaurant almost  10 kilometers southwest of charles de  gaulle airport  flames had consumed the area around the  crash site leaving the investigators a  scattered and scorched debris field to  piece back together  the investigators started by organizing  the accident site to discover clues  about what had happened  clipped trees north of the hotel crash  site suggested that the plane had  crashed while heading south  following along that southward  trajectory the runes of the concorde's  powerful turbojet engines were scattered  on the darkened burnt ground  the site was littered with large impact  marks with pieces of fuselage embedded  in the ground  further south the main cabin was found  alongside the tragic victims of the  crash to the east like the cockpit and  its burned instruments  not all of the instruments survived the  flames and many of them were missing  however a few instruments did survive  the crash and the heat  the investigators noted down the final  readings and the dials they could read  this would hopefully provide crucial  information that would lead to a  revelation as the investigation  continued  as they dug through the remains of the  aircraft they found the flight data  recorder the tool that stores critical  data about the plane's flight record  they also found the cockpit voice  recorder allowing the investigators to  listen to the plane's final moments  in these audio recordings a large bang  is heard shortly after the officer  called out v1 at the same moment the  flight data recorder displayed a sudden  loss of speed and thrust in engine  number two on the left wing followed  closely by its neighbor engine 1.  while engines 1 and 2 were struggling  the data showed that the plane kept  flying with an almost constant angle of  attack without gaining altitude while  the loss of thrust on the left wing  caused the plane to roll  this flight data and the audio  recordings provided a lot of evidence as  to what had happened but it left a  crucial question unanswered what was  that loud noise that preceded the fire  and what caused it  to find more clues the investigators  turned their attention to the runway  even without the data recordings the  scorched concrete painted a vivid  picture of the concord's final moments  it was clear from the burned grass and  damaged lights that the concord had lost  control and veered to the left while  irregular marks in the concrete and  chunks of rubber pointed to a left tire  failure  along the chair trail the plane left  more breadcrumb clues crucial to  continuing the investigation  besides the damaged rubber tire two  other items stood out to the  investigation a mysterious titanium  strip with irregular drilled holes and  an unscorched piece of the plane no  larger than a sheet of printer paper  they were perplexed by the thin metal  strip irregular drilled holes are not  very common in the industry  this led to investigators trying to  match the 42 centimeter long strip to  any component of the concorde but they  couldn't find a match for it anywhere in  the design documents  they concluded that this strip was not  part of the plane and had been there  before the plane took off  logically the next step was to  scrutinize all of the departing flights  on the same runway to identify where  this metal strip could have come from  flight 4590 left at 2 30 pm they scoured  through all of the maintenance and  engineering documents for any plane that  took off after 1pm  one of the many papers they went through  pointed them in the right direction a  mcdonnell douglas dc10 that was  servicing a continental flight that left  a mere five minutes before the air  france flight had a maintenance document  referencing a metallic wear strip  the maintenance documents showed that  the plane had undergone repairs of a  metal wear strip just one month prior in  tel aviv  the same document showed that the  maintenance was not up to manufacturing  standards to confirm the investigators  tracked down the dc-10 and found it was  missing a wear strip with the same  irregular drilled holes the culprit's  fingerprints had been matched to the  scene of the crime  the investigators concluded that a few  minutes before flight 4590 was due to  depart the continental flight dropped  its wear strip on the runway crucially a  32 centimeter long cut was found in the  rubber tire debris left on the runway  while it was tempting to connect this 42  centimeter long metal strip and the cut  found in the tire the investigators  could not assume it was the cause  to confirm investigators built a  simulated concorde a wagon modified to  fit a new concord tire loaded with 25  tons to simulate the weight of the  aircraft and indeed the strip cut the  tyre exactly as seen in the crashed  debris  however this only answers why a tire  would burst  crucially this does not answer how a  tyre burst would cause the eventual  fiery crash  after all this crash was not the first  time the concorde suffered a burst tyre  during takeoff  during its 30-year program the concorde  experienced 57 tire malfunctions 22 of  these events happened during takeoff and  19 were caused by a foreign object in 12  of these events structural damage was  seen and six experienced fuel tank  penetrations but none caused a fire like  the one seen on flight 4590  one event in particular stands out on  the 14th of june 1979 an air france  concord took off from jules airport in  washington dc heading to paris  during the takeoff one of the tires  burst and caused punctures in three of  the tanks  this led to fuel being leaked at a rate  of 4 kilograms per second  luckily the crew noticed the damage and  24 minutes after takeoff they landed  safely back at jules airport  in the report that followed this  incident it was stated that the risk of  fire was low after penetration of the  tanks they stated that the low rate of  fuel leakage coupled with scarce  ignition sources did not pose a  significant risk of fire  they also stated that even in the event  of a leak the location of the tanks  would cause the fuel to disperse rather  than accumulate  the images and videos of the concorde in  flames play in stark contrast to the  findings from this previous  investigation this of course begs the  question what was different about this  flight that led to this infamous picture  to answer that question the  investigators needed to quantify the  fuel leak rate of flight 4590  visual evidence that came from videos  and images of the crash provided the  first clues  using these images the investigators  could estimate the dimensions of the  flames trailing behind the concord's  wing gauging the flame to be 50 meters  long 3 meters in diameter and 10  centimeters thick  they could estimate the rate of fuel  leak needed to sustain such a blaze at  60 kilograms per second  one of the instruments that survived the  crash confirmed this number with the  initial fuel load known some simple  calculations confirmed the fuel leak at  a rate of 60 kilograms per second using  the fuel dial still stuck in its final  position 15 times greater than the 4  kilogram per second leak witnessed in  the previous incident at jules airport  this points to a very different fuel  leak mechanism  here is where the third piece of  evidence from the runway helped the  investigators solve the mystery of the  crash  a single 30 centimeter by 30 centimeter  piece of tank was found on the runway  this piece held the secrets to what  happened to an untrained eye this piece  of metal wouldn't stand out  coated in the concord's white heat  resistant paint on one side and  strengthening struts running along its  length on the other this was enough  detail to pinpoint exactly where in the  plane it came from tank number five  right above the landing gear  next the investigators noted that the  piece was not burned at all indicating  that it dropped before the fire started  to crack the final piece of the puzzle  the investigators needed a material  science expert  to an untrained eye the fracture surface  of a metal tells you little but to an  expert in fracture mechanics it tells  the story  the investigator's report noted that the  ruptures and the thin skin were matte  for the most part the rupture face was  angled at 45 degrees which indicates  that these were static ruptures  to understand the significance of this  passage we need some background on  fracture mechanics  why does a 45 degree rupture face  indicate a static rupture  let's run a couple of examples with  different materials undergoing different  types of stress and see how their  fracture geometries vary the  investigators knew that the type of  aluminium being used in the tank was a  ductile material  if we take a ductile material and apply  stress to it as its name implies the  material sample would deform as the load  increases this leads to a phenomenon  called necking where the material thins  out until it can't stand a larger load  and then fails  this leaves a characteristic 45 degree  angle on the failed piece  often in these types of failures you can  see a cup and ball surface again at a 45  degree angle  these types of surfaces only happen to  ductile materials under tension if  another type of stress would be applied  to the sample say torsional stress then  the material would break with a 90  degree angle  examining the fracture surface of this  piece told the experts working on this  case a story  the hole in the tank wasn't caused by a  puncture by the tire physically piercing  the tank it pointed towards a rupture  caused by a buildup of pressure inside  the tank  the piece of rubber hit the underside of  the wing with enough force to cause a  deformation in the tank  causing the walls of the tank to bend in  while adjacent sections bulged out  this was a serious problem because the  fuel was incompressible and being the  start of the flight the tanks were full  the resulting pressure wave slammed down  into the bulging sections and ejected  the piece of tank found on the runway  and a hole this large was capable of  causing a fuel leak large enough to  match the flames seen in the images of  the tragic accident  the scene was set a poorly positioned  metal strip caused a stressed tire to  burst and slam against the underside of  the wing which in turn led to a massive  pressure wave inside the tank which  ruptured the tank leading to a  catastrophic and tragic loss of power  leading to the plane's uncontrollable  descent  a decade-long legal battle ensued  between air france and continental where  they took turns pointing fingers at each  other finalizing in a 2010 court ruling  that fined continental 1 million euros  plans were made to add kevlar liners to  the fuel tanks to prevent further static  ruptures like this and potential  electric ignition sources were also  moved and reinforced in the area new  damage resilient tires were also  introduced  however these modifications meant little  for the financially struggling concorde  this crash proved to be the final nail  in the coffin for the airline with high  ticket prices and low demand for flights  becoming even lower in the wake of the  crash the concorde proved to be  financially unviable leading to both  british airways and air france to cancel  their supersonic flying programs the end  of an era marked by a tragic crash  i hesitated making this video for  youtube as it deals with the tragic  accident and it's not always clear what  will and won't upset the youtube  demonetization system that's why i moved  all my world war ii related content to  nebula where i can continue making  videos about a subject i am passionate  about without having to worry about  youtube views between my logistics of  d-day in battle of britain's series i  have over three hours of documentaries  you can watch right now  this isn't like the same old boring  history documentaries being remade over  and over again these days repeating  themselves constantly for their 30  minute run times so they can fit enough  ads in this is in-depth research  distilled into three hours of  entertaining and informative  documentaries the same nitty-gritty  detail you've come to enjoy from real  engineering just applied to a different  genre  we will also be sharing additional  content from our upcoming documentary  about spin launch in the coming month as  we have hours of footage that will end  up on the cutting room floor without a  suitable place to share it  we are just one of the creators using  nebula to experiment and make videos  that may not work on youtube there is  real life laura's modern conflict series  wendover productions game show that i  competed in where you will see some of  my usual day-to-day chaotic personality  that i desperately try to hide from you  on this channel  nebula is not just the home of extra  videos it's a proving ground for new  ideas and concepts that helps us as  content creators to experiment and  develop our skills  skills that have bled into these videos  with our 3d animation abilities growing  thanks to the battle of britain series  on top of all that the best and cheapest  way to get access to nebula is by  signing up to curiositystream for the  low price of 14.79 a year that will get  you access to curiosity streams massive  library of award-winning documentaries  and get nebula bundled in for free you  can sign up with the link on screen  right now  or if you're looking for something else  right now you can watch our last video  about spacex's new rocket fuel or watch  real sciences latest video explaining  why crows are so smart  [Music]  you

